## The models

- {% model name="gpt-4o-mini-tts (2025-03-20)" vendor="openai" /%} `gpt-4o-mini-tts-2025-03-20`, OpenAI direct, voice marin. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $12 per million audio tokens plus $0.60 per million text tokens; about 2.1¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="gpt-4o-mini-tts (2025-12-15)" vendor="openai" /%} `gpt-4o-mini-tts-2025-12-15`, OpenAI direct, voice marin. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $12 per million audio tokens plus $0.60 per million text tokens; about 1.9¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="tts-1-hd" vendor="openai" /%} `tts-1-hd`, OpenAI direct, voice nova. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $30 per million characters; about 2.9¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="Gemini 3.1 Flash TTS via OpenRouter" vendor="google" /%} `gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview`, OpenRouter, voice Zephyr. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: not recorded; OpenRouter billed each request; about 4.3¢ per minute of speech in this run, as billed by OpenRouter.
- {% model name="Gemini 3.8 Flash TTS" vendor="google" /%} `gemini-3.8-flash-tts`, Google AI Studio, voice Zephyr. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $0.50 per million text tokens plus $9 per million audio tokens; rises on 2027-01-01; about 1.4¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="Gemini 3.8 Flash-Lite TTS" vendor="google" /%} `gemini-3.8-flash-lite-tts`, Google AI Studio, voice Zephyr. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $0.50 per million text tokens plus $6 per million audio tokens; rises on 2027-01-01; about 1.2¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="Gemini 3.1 Flash TTS" vendor="google" /%} `gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview`, Google AI Studio, voice Zephyr. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $1 per million text tokens plus $20 per million audio tokens; about 3.0¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="MAI-Voice-2" vendor="microsoft" /%} `microsoft/mai-voice-2`, OpenRouter, voice en-US-Harper:MAI-Voice-2. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: not recorded; OpenRouter billed each request; about 1.8¢ per minute of speech in this run, as billed by OpenRouter. Azure's native SSML (<lang>, <phoneme>, <prosody>, <break>, mid-text style changes) is not reachable through OpenRouter and was not tested.
- {% model name="MAI-Voice-2 Flash" vendor="microsoft" /%} `microsoft/mai-voice-2-flash`, OpenRouter, voice en-US-Harper:MAI-Voice-2. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: not recorded; OpenRouter billed each request; about 1.2¢ per minute of speech in this run, as billed by OpenRouter. Azure's native SSML (<lang>, <phoneme>, <prosody>, <break>, mid-text style changes) is not reachable through OpenRouter and was not tested.
- {% model name="Fish Audio S2.1 Pro" vendor="fish" /%} `fish-audio/s2.1-pro`, OpenRouter, voice Hannah (Fish official, 9a9cf477…). Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: not recorded; OpenRouter billed each request; about 1.5¢ per minute of speech in this run, as billed by OpenRouter.
- {% model name="MiniMax Speech 2.8 Turbo" vendor="minimax" /%} `minimax/speech-2.8-turbo`, OpenRouter, voice English_expressive_narrator. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: not recorded; OpenRouter billed each request; about 4.8¢ per minute of speech in this run, as billed by OpenRouter.
- {% model name="ElevenLabs v3" vendor="elevenlabs" /%} `eleven_v3`, ElevenLabs direct, voice Chris. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: 1 credit per character; the Creator plan is $22 for 220,000 characters; about 10.1¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="ElevenLabs Flash v2.5" vendor="elevenlabs" /%} `eleven_flash_v2_5`, ElevenLabs direct, voice Chris. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $0.05 per 1,000 characters; about 5.3¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="Cartesia Sonic 3.6" vendor="cartesia" /%} `sonic-3.6`, Cartesia direct, voice Tessa. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: about 1 credit per character; the Startup plan is $49 for 1.25 million credits; about 4.1¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="Cartesia Sonic Turbo" vendor="cartesia" /%} `sonic-turbo`, Cartesia direct, voice Tessa. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: about 1 credit per character; the Startup plan is $49 for 1.25 million credits; about 4.3¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="Deepgram Aura-2" vendor="deepgram" /%} `aura-2-thalia-en`, Deepgram direct, voice aura-2-thalia-en. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $0.030 per 1,000 characters; about 2.6¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price.
- {% model name="Deepgram Flux" vendor="deepgram" /%} `flux-hannah-en`, Deepgram direct, voice flux-hannah-en. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $0.045 per 1,000 characters; about 3.7¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price. Flux has no style channel by design; it infers delivery from context.
- {% model name="Grok Voice TTS" vendor="xai" /%} `x-ai/grok-voice-tts-1.0`, OpenRouter, voice eve. Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: not recorded; OpenRouter billed each request; about 1.3¢ per minute of speech in this run, as billed by OpenRouter. Inline tags are limited to events and spans ([pause], <soft>, <slow>, <whisper>); there is no emotion channel.
- {% model name="Voxtral TTS 3" vendor="mistral" /%} `voxtral-mini-tts-3-0`, Mistral direct, voice Sarah (voice_478). Rendered 2026-10-24. List price: $13 per million characters, tags included; about 1.4¢ per minute of speech in this run, at list price. Inline [emotion=…] (34 values) and [pace=…] tags only; no instruction field (style instructions not available at launch). Pronunciation fixes go in a request-level phoneme_hints map of IPA phones, not in the text.

Each model used one voice from its provider's standard list, named above. A
different voice of the same model can sound quite different, so a take shows
the model with that voice, not the model at its best. Cost per minute comes from every
successful take in the run, including pacing takes not on this page: for models reached through OpenRouter, the amount OpenRouter
billed; for the others, the list price applied to what was sent (characters,
or for Gemini on Google's API, the token counts the API returned).

## How it was tested

Each model read seven texts. Each text tests one thing a voice for the box
must do:

- **Pacing:** a long sentence with asides and a list in parentheses, read
  plain only: it tests the model's own phrasing. Rendered five times per
  model, because pauses vary from take to take. The page plays the take of
  middle length and gives the range of lengths. Three takes are enough for a
  new model.
- **Code-switching:** whole phrases in Spanish, German, and French inside an
  English sentence.
- **Single German words:** two German words inside an English sentence.
- **Sarcasm:** words that mean the opposite of what they say. Nothing in the
  text marks it, so only direction can make it sound sarcastic.
- **Relief, anger, exhaustion:** a mood the words already carry, to hear
  whether the model finds it without help.

Every model read every text **plain**: the text alone, with no direction.
Models that accept direction also read some texts **directed**, and a few
read them with **markup** (pauses, sighs, speed changes) as well. Each
backend takes direction in its own way, so the directed takes are not one
experiment repeated. Under each directed take, the page shows exactly what
was sent. The ways seen here:

- **A prose instruction in a separate field.** OpenAI's gpt-4o-mini-tts
  (`instructions`) and Gemini 3.8 (`speech_metadata.style`). The direction is
  not part of the text, so the model cannot read it aloud. tts-1-hd ignores
  instructions.
- **Prose or tags inside the text.** Gemini 3.1 takes a prose direction in
  front of the text, and free-form bracket tags such as `[sarcastically]`
  anywhere in it. ElevenLabs v3 and Fish Audio take free-form bracket tags.
  Direction inside the text can be read aloud as words. Gemini 3.8 reads
  bracket tags aloud, so the tag style that works on 3.1 fails on 3.8.
- **A value from a fixed list.** Cartesia (`generation_config.emotion`),
  MiniMax (an emotion option), and MAI-Voice-2 through OpenRouter (a `style`
  such as `angry` or `relieved`, with nothing for sarcasm or tiredness). One
  value applies to the whole take. Voxtral takes fixed `[emotion=…]` and
  `[pace=…]` tags inside the text, and pronunciation as IPA phones in a
  separate `phoneme_hints` field.
- **Events and spans.** Grok takes pauses, sighs, and spans such as
  `<slow>…</slow>` and `<soft>…</soft>`, but no emotion.
- **Nothing.** Deepgram Aura-2 and Flux and ElevenLabs Flash take no
  direction, so they read every text plain only. Flux infers delivery from the
  text by design.

Some controls were not tried: Azure's full SSML for MAI-Voice-2 (not
reachable through OpenRouter), ElevenLabs voice settings and pronunciation
dictionaries, Cartesia pronunciation dictionaries, and Voxtral's
`expressiveness`.

**The transcript check.** The first successful take of each model, text, and
variant was sent to a speech-to-text model. Its transcript was searched for
words of the direction that are not in the text, which means the model read
its direction aloud. The other differences between transcript and text are
not shown: nearly all were the transcriber's ("40" for "forty", the foreign
phrases respelled). The Voxtral takes were transcribed with OpenAI's
gpt-4o-transcribe. The transcriber used for the September takes is not
recorded.

The renders, the transcript check, and the script that rendered the Voxtral
takes are in the repository at
[research/tts-model-comparison](https://github.com/ianb/beebox/tree/main/research/tts-model-comparison).

## Direction read aloud

Direction inside the text can be spoken as words. The transcript check found
it in these takes. All are Gemini; Gemini 3.8 reads bracket tags aloud, and
Gemini 3.1 can read a prose direction in front of the text. The check sees
only words the transcriber wrote down, so a tag read aloud in a way the
transcriber dropped is not listed.

- {% model name="Deepgram Aura-2" vendor="deepgram" /%} [X · Word stress](#x-word-stress), **Markdown \*she\***. **Read Markdown aloud:** “star”, “star”. Take [X-she-italic-aura2](#X-she-italic-aura2).
- {% model name="Cartesia Sonic Turbo" vendor="cartesia" /%} [E · Worked math](#e-worked-math), **Markdown \_then\_**. **Read Markdown aloud:** “underscore”, “underscore”. Take [E-then-italic-sonicturbo](#E-then-italic-sonicturbo).

